============================================================
EXAMPLE 1: FIFA-STYLE 5-TEAM GROUP
============================================================

Teams: Brazil > France > Japan > Germany > Costa Rica (with upset)
H(T) = 3 Hamiltonian paths
E[H] = 7.5 for random 5-team tournament
CV = sqrt(2/5) = 0.632
Z-score = -0.95
Interpretation: Within random noise

Perfect hierarchy: H=1, Z=-1.37
Cyclic tournament: H=15, Z=1.58

============================================================
EXAMPLE 2: CHESS 6-PLAYER CANDIDATES
============================================================

Strong hierarchy with competitive middle: H=3, Z=-1.50
E[H] = 22.5, CV = 0.577

Competitive with upsets: H=13, Z=-0.73

============================================================
EXAMPLE 3: WHEN DO RANKINGS BECOME MEANINGFUL?
============================================================

For a ranking to be 'real' (Z > 2), the tournament must have
H(T) > E[H] * (1 + 2*CV) = E[H] * (1 + 2*sqrt(2/n)).

  n    E[H]          CV      Threshold H/E[H]
  --------------------------------------------------
    4            3.0   0.7071   2.4142
    5            7.5   0.6325   2.2649
    6           22.5   0.5774   2.1547
    8          315.0   0.5000   2.0000
   10         7087.5   0.4472   1.8944
   16    638512875.0   0.3536   1.7071
   20   4640392319062.5   0.3162   1.6325
   32   122529844256906545612193792.0   0.2500   1.5000

For n=32 teams: any H within 50% of E[H] is noise.
Only H > 1.50*E[H] indicates real skill differences.

============================================================
EXAMPLE 4: LEAGUE COMPETITIVENESS INDEX
============================================================

L(T) = H(T)/E[H]: normalized Hamiltonian path count.
  L >> 1: clear hierarchy (easy to rank)
  L ~ 1: competitive (rankings are noise)
  L << 1: paradoxical (many contradictions)

Random 5-team tournaments (1000 samples):
  Mean L: 1.016 (should be ~1)
  Std L:  0.570 (should be ~0.632)
  Min L:  0.133
  Max L:  2.000
  P(L > 1.5): 19.1%
  P(L > 2.0): 0.0%

============================================================
EXAMPLE 5: PRODUCT RANKING (A/B TESTING)
============================================================

7 products compared pairwise by users.
Each pair: which product is preferred?
The tournament T encodes all pairwise preferences.

E[H] = 78.8 for n=7
CV = 0.535
sigma = 42.1

If your A/B test gives H = 189 (maximum possible):
  Z = 2.62 — extremely significant ranking
If your A/B test gives H = 79 (near random):
  Z = 0.01 — no significant ranking
If your A/B test gives H = 120 (moderate):
  Z = 0.98 — moderate evidence of ranking
